WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:05.415
A rating form organizes a judgment; it does not make that judgment automatically fair.

2
00:00:05.615 --> 00:00:09.800
An essay gives context but can vary with the evaluator's writing skill.

3
00:00:10.000 --> 00:00:16.112
A graphic rating scale is compact, yet words such as good may mean different things to different supervisors.

4
00:00:16.312 --> 00:00:21.636
Behaviorally anchored rating scales connect levels to concrete examples of job behavior.

5
00:00:21.836 --> 00:00:28.624
For registration work, an anchor might describe checking the record, explaining options, and using the referral route.

6
00:00:28.824 --> 00:00:34.162
Developing credible anchors takes time and participation from people who know the job.

7
00:00:34.362 --> 00:00:36.821
Ranking compares employees with one another.

8
00:00:37.021 --> 00:00:45.353
Forced distribution places predetermined proportions into rating categories, which can misrepresent the actual performance of a small team.

9
00:00:45.553 --> 00:00:47.386
No method fits every setting.

10
00:00:47.586 --> 00:00:51.635
Combining a scale with a short evidence-based explanation may help.

11
00:00:51.835 --> 00:00:58.537
Also distinguish the method from the source of feedback: supervisor, self, peers, or multiple perspectives.

12
00:00:58.737 --> 00:01:05.535
More raters can broaden the view, but they do not automatically remove bias or make every comment equally relevant.
